zsh: `&&` and `||` with nothing after them, and a lone `;` in arrays

After `&&` or `||` and any newlines or `;`, zsh parses the rest of the
list only if a command can start there. If none can, the list ends after
the commands before the operator, which run as if it were not there:

	a &&             # runs a, at the end of the input of zsh -c, a
	                 # script or a sourced file alike
	{ a || }         # runs a
	if a && then b; fi
	case x in x) a && ;; esac

The token after the operator still has to be valid where it stands, so
`a && }` at the top level fails at `}` as before, and `&`, `|`, `|&`,
`&&` and `||` right after the operator remain errors, as in zsh. zsh 5.9
runs every example above, with the exit status of the last command it
ran. A pipe, `|` or `|&`, with nothing after it stays an error, as in zsh.

zsh's lexer also turns a `;` inside the parentheses of an array
assignment into the separator token that a newline gives, so
`a=(x ; y)`, `a=(x;y)` and `a=(;)` assign the words without the `;`,
while `;;` or `&` there are errors. `[[ ]]` still rejects a `;`, as zsh
does.

Reproducer, before this change:

	$ printf 'a &&' | shfmt -ln zsh
	<standard input>:1:3: `&&` must be followed by a statement
	$ printf 'a=(x ; y)\n' | shfmt -ln zsh
	<standard input>:1:6: array element values must be words

With LangZsh, such an operator gets an empty BinaryCmd.Y statement at
the end of the operator, and array literals skip any number of `;` and
newlines between elements. The test sanity checker allows a statement
that starts at the end of the input.

Part of https://github.com/mvdan/sh/issues/120.

